//
// @PLN153 phase 1 — the REFUSE half of @FR-N-Decl's pair: a DECLARED non-null local forbids a later nullable write.
//
// The pair exists so the citation at the rule's home is a receipt for a measured
// refusal, not a comment: `rule_tags.py check` counts the citation, this file is what
// makes it honest.  Its expectation is the diagnostic's own text, pinned as a substring.
//
// "Forbids" with `(N-Store)`'s severity: the declaration is a commitment, so `a` stays an
// `integer` (it does NOT widen the way the inferred twin in `153-n-rules-hold` does) and the
// write is reported as the store it is — a WARNING at full width, the store proceeding.
//
// @falsified-at: 8498fdf1 → here (hand-measured, a warning-only channel): the parent build
//   REFUSES this program (`cannot change type from integer to integer?`), so the warning
//   below is unmatched and the file fails there.
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `integer?` is stored into the local `a` of the non-null type `integer`
fn f(v: vector<integer>, i: integer) -> integer { a: integer = 2; a = v[i]; return a; }
fn main() {
  assert(f([1, 2], 5) == null, "the slot holds the null: {f([1, 2], 5)}");
  assert(f([1, 2], 0) == 1, "an in-range read is the value: {f([1, 2], 0)}");
  println("declared local keeps its type");
}
